p('No useful parameter regime for the proposed QC-GHP stopping bound','Title2')
p('24 October 2026 · Universal negative answer · L-F16-stopping-bound','Small2')
p('<b>Abstract.</b> We prove that no positive-dimensional binary biregular parameter choice in the scalar-factor QC-GHP model used by Abiad and Castriota’s Proposition 5.1 yields a stopping-distance guarantee exceeding two. With at least two row blocks, an unavoidable scalar-block spectral contribution contradicts the required inequality. With one row block, the inequality forces density above three quarters and a large root in (1,2]. Positive column weight excludes the small-size branch, but already gives the same integer lower bound. The raw spectral inequality can hold: a checked dense CSS example attains it with stopping distance two. This is a universal impossibility proof for useful parameter choices, not a failed search or a counterexample to the proposition’s inequality. All proofs and matrix/root translations are checked in Lean.')
h('Question, source and answer')
table([['Question / source', 'Answer / evidence'], ['Original parameter question: Abiad-Castriota2026, Prop.5.1 / Remark5.2 / §6', 'Answered negatively in its stated model: no useful root above2. Universal checked nonexistence theorem.'], ['Stronger replacement-bound direction in the same §6', 'Still open; distinct from the answered parameter question.']],[187,305])
p('Documentation revision: full Lean URLs, actual proof excerpts, dependency guide and complete calendar-delta ledger are supplied in Appendices A-C. No existing proof or source-question count changes; the additional dense-source bridge is explicitly identified.','Small2')
page()
h('1. Source question and precise negative answer')
p('Aida Abiad and Nichola Castriota [1], 15 July 2026, arXiv:2607.13666v1, Remark 5.2 and §6, ask for QC-GHP design parameters making Proposition 5.1 informative, and separately for a stronger spectral bound. We answer the useful-parameter component negatively for the construction stated in that paper. Its §3.2 specializes to r₂=n₂=1, describing this as “used throughout this paper”; Proposition 5.1 uses Theorem 4.1’s corresponding scalar-shift Fourier matrices. General two-matrix or nonabelian products are extensions beyond this proposition’s setup, rather than missing parameter cases in the theorem below.')
p('Let r,n₁,ℓ,γ be positive integers, k a natural number, and m=rℓ≥2. Let A be the expanded rℓ-by-n₁ℓ first block and B the ℓ-by-ℓ scalar circulant. Assume the real expanded matrix H=[A | Iᵣ⊗Bᵀ] has entries zero or one, column weight γ and row weight k. Put s=σ₂(H)², counting Gram eigenvalues in descending order with multiplicity. Define the original source roots by')
eq('a±=(m/γ)(1±√(1−2s/γ)).')
p('<b>Theorem 1 (universal obstruction).</b> There are no such parameters and matrices satisfying both s&lt;γ/2 and a₊&gt;2. More precisely, if s&lt;γ/2 then r=1 and 1&lt;a₊≤2. Every nonempty stopping support S satisfies |S|≥2, a₋&lt;|S| and a₊≤|S|. Thus the small-size branch is excluded, while the resulting integer guarantee is exactly two and follows already from positive column weight.')
p('All positive row-block counts and both finite and asymptotic parameter choices are covered. Circulancy, CSS commutation, girth and connectivity are unnecessary for the obstruction: the formal theorem proves it for a larger matrix class. Positive dimensions, positive column degree and m≥2 are explicit hypotheses. Empty first factors, γ=0 and matrices without a second singular value are outside this statement. No logical-distance claim is made.','Small2')
page();h('2. Proof of the universal obstruction')
p('<b>Case r≥2.</b> At zero Fourier frequency the repeated scalar block contributes γ²Iᵣ to a positive semidefinite Gram matrix. At least two eigenvalues of this block are at least γ²; removing one top eigenvalue leaves s≥γ². For positive integral γ this contradicts s&lt;γ/2. The Lean proof works directly with the expanded matrix: on vectors constant within row fibers the scalar block supplies energy γ² times squared norm, and the first block adds a nonnegative quantity. In a two-dimensional subspace, a nonzero vector orthogonal to a top Gram eigenvector has energy at most s times squared norm. This also covers repeated top eigenvalues.')
p('<b>Case r=1.</b> Now m=ℓ and N=qm with q=n₁+1≥2. Edge counting gives k=qγ, and binary column regularity gives γ≤m. The trace and a weighted Cauchy–Schwarz bound give')
eq('k(m−γ) ≤ (m−1)s. &nbsp; (T)')
p('Indeed tr(HHᵀ)=mk, its largest eigenvalue is at most γk, and every other eigenvalue is at most s. Subtracting the largest from the trace proves (T). Combining it with the strict gap yields γ&gt;m−(m−1)/(2q)≥(3m+1)/4, so the surviving matrices have column density above three quarters.')
p('To bound the root, multiply (T) by γ. Since q≥2 and m²≥γ(m−1), and since s≥0,')
eq('sm² ≥ 2γ²(m−γ),<br/>1−2s/γ ≤ (2γ/m−1)².')
p('The density estimate makes 2γ/m−1 nonnegative. Taking nonnegative square roots proves a₊≤2. Strict positivity of the discriminant and m/γ≥1 imply a₊&gt;1. The same hypotheses give 0≤a₋&lt;2. A singleton support cannot be stopping because its positive column contains a neighboring check of induced degree one. Hence |S|≥2 excludes the entire small-size branch and already implies |S|≥a₊. These two cases exhaust every positive r. □')
h('3. Raw inequality and the separate branch requirement')
p('Proposition 5.1 gives a(m−aγ/2)≤sm²/γ². In the strict-gap regime it yields the disjunction a≤a₋ or a≥a₊; exclusion of the small branch is an additional requirement. Theorem 1 explicitly checks that exclusion and then shows the large-root conclusion adds nothing beyond |S|≥2. Increasing column weight or choosing better constituent expanders cannot change this conclusion within the stated model.')
p('The previously implicit source identification is now checked explicitly: A and B are the length-two circulants with support {0,1}; HX=[A|Bᵀ] and companion HZ=[B|Aᵀ] become the displayed matrices under proved row/column bijections. DenseSourceBridge.source_certificate combines the actual degrees, spectrum, roots, CSS and stopping statements.','Small2')
p('<b>Checked example.</b> The all-ones 2-by-4 pair H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub> is a scalar-factor CSS example with γ=2, k=4, s=0, a₋=0 and a₊=2. Its stopping distance is two and H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=0 over F₂. Thus asserting that the raw spectral inequality never holds would be false. The proved nonexistence concerns a useful improvement beyond two. This does not refute the proposition’s inequality.')
page();h('4. Checked statements and proof coverage')
p('The new theorem below combines the previously checked row-block cases. The matrix, degree, stopping and original-radical bridges are proved, not assumed as spectral-envelope hypotheses. Here eigs H 1 means the second ordered eigenvalue of the actual real HHᵀ.')
eq('no_parameters_give_source_improvement :<br/>∀ positive r, γ and positive finite dimensions,<br/>∀ binary (γ,k)-biregular H=[A | Iᵣ⊗Bᵀ], m≥2,<br/>¬(eigs H 1&lt;γ/2 ∧ 2&lt;sourceLargeRoot m γ (eigs H 1)).')
p('The companion theorem source_parameter_question_negative also concludes r=1, 1&lt;a₊≤2 and, for EVERY nonempty stopping support, |S|≥2, a₋&lt;|S| and a₊≤|S|. The exact Lean signatures, including nonempty first-factor and fiber index types, appear in the axiom audit.')
table([['Proof or bridge','Checked declaration / source'],['Actual real Gram spectrum, trace and energy','Spectral.lean: energy_eq_gram, energy_eigenbasis, energy_le_of_coeff_zero, eigs_trace'],['Binary biregular trace bound and dimensions','Spectral.lean: binary_weighted_cauchy, regular_trace_obstruction, binary_column_sum_le_card, regular_edge_count, one_row_cardinal_obstruction'],['Every r≥2: scalar block obstruction','Spectral.lean: scalarFactor_energy_lower, replicated_scalar_second_lower, qc_scalar_factor_no_source_regime'],['Original radical translation and r=1 roots','Spectral.lean: sourceLargeRoot_eq, sourceSmallRoot_eq, one_row_density, one_row_large_root_le_two, one_row_roots'],['Stopping definition and small branch exclusion','Spectral.lean: IsStopping, stopping_card_ge_two, scalar_one_row_source_obstruction'],['Dense source construction, CSS and exact stopping distance','DenseSourceBridge.lean: source_certificate; Spectral.lean: denseExample_second, denseExample_css, denseExample_source_certificate, denseExample_minimum'],['All positive r; direct nonexistence statement','SourceQuestionAnswer.lean: source_parameter_question_negative, no_parameters_give_source_improvement']],[185,307])
